/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/linux-plus.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addQuestions("linux-plus", [["lx1",0,1,"A new USB network adapter is not working, and the admin wants to confirm whether its driver module is currently loaded in the running kernel. Which command shows this?",["modinfo","depmod","lsmod","insmod"],2,"lsmod lists the modules loaded right now (it reads /proc/modules). modinfo only describes a module file on disk, whether or not it is loaded.","1.0 System management: kernel modules",["modinfo describes a module file on disk (its parameters, dependencies and path) whether or not it is loaded; it does not report the running kernel's loaded modules.","depmod rebuilds the modules.dep dependency database; it does not list which modules are currently loaded.",null,"insmod inserts a single module file into the kernel; it loads a module rather than reporting what is already loaded."]],["lx2",0,1,"On a UEFI server, which component loads the Linux kernel and the initramfs into memory during boot?",["systemd running as PID 1","The dracut utility","The /etc/fstab file","The GRUB2 boot loader"],3,"Firmware starts the boot loader (GRUB2 or shim + GRUB2), which loads the kernel and initramfs. systemd starts only after the kernel is running; dracut builds initramfs images but does not boot them.","1.0 System management: boot process",["systemd runs as PID 1 only after the kernel is already running, so it cannot be what loads the kernel and initramfs.","dracut builds initramfs images on disk ahead of time; it does not load them into memory during boot.","/etc/fstab lists filesystems to mount once the system is running; it plays no part in loading the kernel.",null]],["lx3",0,1,"After adding a storage driver that must be available at boot on a RHEL-family server, the admin needs to rebuild the initial RAM disk. Which command does this?",["grub2-mkconfig -o /boot/grub2/grub.cfg","depmod -a","dracut -f","mkfs.ext4 /boot"],2,"dracut -f regenerates the initramfs for the running kernel. grub2-mkconfig rebuilds the boot menu, not the initramfs, so the new driver would still be missing early in boot.","1.0 System management: boot process",["grub2-mkconfig rebuilds the GRUB boot menu, not the initramfs, so the new storage driver would still be absent early in boot.","depmod -a rebuilds the module dependency map; it does not regenerate the initramfs image.",null,"mkfs.ext4 /boot formats the boot partition, erasing it, and has nothing to do with rebuilding the initramfs."]],["lx4",0,1,"According to the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard, where should an application write its variable log files?",["/usr/log","/var/log","/etc/log","/opt/log"],1,"/var holds variable data such as logs, spools and caches. /etc is for configuration and /usr is meant to be read-only shared program data.","1.0 System management: filesystem hierarchy",["/usr is meant to be read-only shared program data and has no standard directory for variable logs.",null,"/etc holds configuration files, not variable log data, and there is no standard /etc/log.","/opt is for self-contained add-on software packages, not for system log files."]],["lx5",0,1,"An admin wants to mount a new disk in /etc/fstab by UUID so the entry survives device name changes. Which command shows the partition's UUID?",["df -h /dev/sdc1","mount -a /dev/sdc1","blkid /dev/sdc1","fdisk -n /dev/sdc1"],2,"blkid prints the UUID, label and filesystem type of a block device (lsblk -f works too). df shows usage of mounted filesystems only and does not print UUIDs.","1.0 System management: storage",["df -h reports usage of mounted filesystems and does not print device UUIDs.","mount -a mounts all fstab entries; it is not a UUID query and this syntax is invalid.",null,"fdisk has no -n action; it edits partition tables and does not print UUIDs."]],["lx6",0,1,"The /data logical volume in volume group vg_data is almost full. A new disk, /dev/sdc, has been attached. Which sequence adds the space to /data with the least disruption?",["vgcreate vg_new /dev/sdc; lvcreate; mkfs the new LV","fdisk to merge /dev/sdc into the LV, then run resize2fs","pvcreate /dev/sdc; vgextend vg_data /dev/sdc; lvextend -r","mdadm --create with /dev/sdc, then mount it over /data"],2,"Initialize the disk as a physical volume, add it to the existing volume group, then grow the LV; -r resizes the filesystem in the same step. Creating a new VG gives a separate volume instead of growing /data.","1.0 System management: LVM",["Creating a new volume group gives a separate volume rather than growing the existing /data logical volume.","fdisk cannot merge a disk into a logical volume, and resize2fs needs a larger LV to grow into first.",null,"mdadm --create builds a RAID array, and mounting it over /data hides the existing data instead of extending it."]],["lx7",0,1,"An XFS filesystem on a logical volume must be made 20 GB smaller. What is the supported approach?",["Run xfs_growfs -D with a smaller block count","Unmount it and run resize2fs with the new size","Back it up, recreate it smaller, and restore","Run lvreduce -r on it while it stays mounted"],2,"XFS can grow but cannot shrink. The only way is back up, recreate a smaller filesystem and restore. resize2fs works on ext2/3/4, not XFS.","1.0 System management: filesystems",["xfs_growfs only grows an XFS filesystem; there is no supported way to shrink XFS with it.","resize2fs works on ext2/3/4 filesystems, not XFS.",null,"XFS cannot shrink, and running lvreduce on a mounted volume with a filesystem that can't shrink would corrupt data."]],["lx8",0,1,"Running ls -li in a directory shows: \"1311 -rw-r--r-- 2 app app 812 data.txt\" and \"1311 -rw-r--r-- 2 app app 812 report.txt\". What is the relationship between the two files?",["report.txt is a symbolic link to data.txt","They are hard links to the same inode","They are copies made with cp -p","They live on two different filesystems"],1,"Both names share inode 1311 and the link count is 2, which is what a hard link looks like. A symbolic link would have its own inode, an l file type and an arrow to its target.","1.0 System management: files and links",["A symbolic link has its own separate inode and an 'l' file type with an arrow to its target, not a shared inode number.",null,"Copies made with cp -p would each have a different inode number; here both names share inode 1311.","A shared inode number means the files are on the same filesystem; hard links cannot span filesystems."]],["lx9",0,1,"Which command creates a gzip-compressed archive of /etc that keeps file permissions?",["tar -czpf etc.tar.gz /etc","tar -xzpf etc.tar.gz /etc","tar -tzvf etc.tar.gz /etc","tar -cjpf etc.tar.gz /etc"],0,"-c creates, -z uses gzip, -p keeps permissions and -f names the archive. -x would extract and -j would compress with bzip2 despite the .gz name.","1.0 System management: backup and restore",[null,"-x extracts an archive rather than creating one, so it would not build etc.tar.gz.","-t only lists the contents of an archive; it does not create one.","-j compresses with bzip2, not gzip, which is inconsistent with the .gz name."]],["lx10",0,1,"A web root must be mirrored nightly to host backup so files deleted from the source are also removed from the copy. Which command does this?",["scp -r /srv/web backup:/srv/web","cp -au /srv/web backup:/srv/web","rsync -av --delete /srv/web/ backup:/srv/web/","rsync -av --remove-source-files /srv/web/ backup:/srv/web/"],2,"rsync --delete removes files from the destination that no longer exist in the source, giving a true mirror. --remove-source-files deletes the source files after copying, which destroys the original.","1.0 System management: backup and restore",["scp -r copies recursively but never removes files deleted from the source, so it does not produce a true mirror.","cp cannot write to a remote host:path like that, and it has no delete-extraneous behavior.",null,"--remove-source-files deletes the source files after copying, destroying the original web root."]],["lx11",0,1,"Before replacing a failing disk, an admin wants a block-level image of all of /dev/sdb saved to /backup. Which command does this?",["dd if=/backup/sdb.img of=/dev/sdb bs=4M","tar -cf /backup/sdb.img /dev/sdb","cp --sparse=always /dev/sdb1 /backup/","dd if=/dev/sdb of=/backup/sdb.img bs=4M"],3,"dd reads the input device (if=) and writes it to the output file (of=). Swapping if and of would overwrite the disk with the image, the opposite of what is needed.","1.0 System management: backup and restore",["This has if= and of= reversed, so it would overwrite /dev/sdb with the image file rather than back the disk up.","tar archives files from a filesystem; it cannot image a raw block device like /dev/sdb.","cp --sparse copies only one partition (sdb1) and is not a raw block-level imaging tool for the whole disk.",null]],["lx12",0,1,"On a server managed by NetworkManager, which command sets a static IPv4 address that survives a reboot?",["ip addr add 10.0.0.5/24 dev eth0 && ip link set dev eth0 up","nmcli con mod eth0 ipv4.addresses 10.0.0.5/24 ipv4.method manual","ifconfig eth0 10.0.0.5 netmask 255.255.255.0","echo '10.0.0.5 eth0' >> /etc/hosts"],1,"nmcli connection modify saves the setting in the connection profile, so it persists. ip addr add changes only the running kernel state and is lost at reboot or when the connection restarts.","1.0 System management: network configuration",["ip addr add changes only the running kernel state and is lost at reboot or when the connection restarts.",null,"ifconfig is deprecated on NetworkManager systems and its changes are also non-persistent.","/etc/hosts maps names to IP addresses; it does not assign an address to an interface."]],["lx13",0,1,"An admin needs to rename a server to web01 permanently. Which command should be used?",["hostname web01","hostnamectl set-hostname web01","echo web01 > /proc/sys/kernel/hostname","echo web01 >> /etc/hosts"],1,"hostnamectl set-hostname writes /etc/hostname and updates the running name. The hostname command and /proc change only the running value until the next reboot.","1.0 System management: network configuration",["The hostname command sets only the running name, which reverts at the next reboot.",null,"Writing to /proc/sys/kernel/hostname changes the runtime value only and does not persist.","Appending to /etc/hosts adds a name-to-IP mapping; it does not set the system hostname."]],["lx14",0,1,"A server keeps resolving app.local through DNS even though /etc/hosts has a different entry for it. Which file controls the order in which these sources are checked?",["/etc/resolv.conf","/etc/nsswitch.conf","/etc/hosts.allow","/etc/sysconfig/network"],1,"The hosts: line in /etc/nsswitch.conf sets the lookup order (for example files dns). resolv.conf lists DNS servers and search domains but does not decide whether /etc/hosts is read first.","1.0 System management: network configuration",["/etc/resolv.conf lists DNS servers and search domains but does not control whether /etc/hosts is consulted first.",null,"/etc/hosts.allow is part of TCP wrappers access control and has nothing to do with name resolution order.","/etc/sysconfig/network holds settings like the hostname and gateway, not the lookup source order."]],["lx15",0,1,"Which command sends both standard output and standard error of cmd to log.txt?",["cmd > log.txt 2>&1","cmd 2>&1 > log.txt","cmd | tee log.txt","cmd 2> log.txt"],0,"Redirections are processed left to right: stdout goes to the file, then stderr is pointed at the same place. With 2>&1 first, stderr is copied to the terminal before stdout moves, so errors still reach the screen.","1.0 System management: shell operations",[null,"With 2>&1 placed first, stderr is pointed at the terminal (the current stdout) before stdout is redirected, so errors do not land in the file.","tee writes stdout to the file and the screen but does not capture stderr at all.","2> log.txt redirects only standard error, leaving standard output going to the terminal."]],["lx16",0,1,"What does cut -d: -f1,7 print for the line \"root:x:0:0:root:/root:/bin/bash\"?",["root /bin/bash","root:x:0:0","/root:/bin/bash","root:/bin/bash"],3,"cut splits on the colon delimiter and prints fields 1 and 7, joined with the same delimiter. Field 6 is /root, the home directory, not field 7.","1.0 System management: shell operations",["cut joins the selected fields with the delimiter (a colon here), not a space.","root:x:0:0 is fields 1 through 4, not fields 1 and 7.","/root:/bin/bash is fields 6 and 7; field 1 is root, not /root.",null]],["lx17",0,1,"A shell variable APP_ENV=prod is set at the prompt, but a script started from that shell sees it as empty. What fixes this?",["set APP_ENV=prod","export APP_ENV=prod","local APP_ENV=prod","alias APP_ENV=prod"],1,"export marks the variable for the environment of child processes. Without it, the variable is shell-local; local only works inside functions and alias defines a command shortcut.","1.0 System management: shell operations",["Using set with an assignment does not export the variable; set manages shell options and positional parameters.",null,"local only works inside a function and still does not export a variable to child processes.","alias defines a command shortcut, not an environment variable."]],["lx18",0,1,"On a KVM host, which command lists every defined virtual machine, including ones that are shut off?",["qemu-img info --all","virsh list --all","virt-install --list","lsmod | grep kvm"],1,"virsh list shows running domains; --all adds inactive ones. lsmod | grep kvm only confirms the KVM modules are loaded.","1.0 System management: virtualization",["qemu-img info reports on a disk image file, not on defined virtual machines, and has no --all VM listing.",null,"virt-install creates new VMs; it has no --list option for existing domains.","lsmod | grep kvm only confirms that the KVM kernel modules are loaded."]],["lx19",0,1,"A VM disk image should grow only as data is written and should support internal snapshots. Which image format fits?",["raw","ISO 9660","tar.gz","qcow2"],3,"qcow2 (QEMU copy-on-write) is thin-provisioned and supports snapshots and backing files. Raw images are simple and fast but have no built-in snapshot support.","1.0 System management: virtualization",["A raw image is full-size with no thin provisioning and no built-in snapshot support.","ISO 9660 is a read-only optical disc filesystem format, not a growable writable VM disk.","tar.gz is a compressed archive format, not a bootable VM disk image.",null]],["lx20",0,1,"Which command finds files under /var larger than 100 MB that were modified in the last 7 days?",["find /var -size -100M -mtime +7","find /var -size +100M -mtime -7","find /var -size 100M -atime 7","locate /var -size +100M -mtime -7"],1,"+100M means larger than 100 MiB and -mtime -7 means modified less than 7 days ago. Reversing the signs finds small, old files; locate searches a name database and has no size test.","1.0 System management: files and directories",["-size -100M finds files smaller than 100 MiB and -mtime +7 finds files modified more than 7 days ago, both the reverse of what is wanted.",null,"-size 100M matches files of exactly 100 MiB and -atime uses access time, not the size-over-100M / recently-modified criteria.","locate searches a filename database and has no -size or -mtime tests."]],["lx21",0,1,"A USB backup disk listed in /etc/fstab is sometimes unplugged, and the server then drops to emergency mode at boot. Which mount option prevents this?",["noexec","defaults","nofail","sync"],2,"nofail tells systemd not to fail the boot if the device is missing. noexec only blocks running programs from the filesystem.","1.0 System management: storage",["noexec only blocks executing programs from the filesystem; it does not affect boot behavior when the device is missing.","defaults enables the standard options (rw, suid, dev, exec, auto, nouser, async) and still fails the boot if the device is absent.",null,"sync forces synchronous I/O to the filesystem and does nothing about a missing device at boot."]],["lx22",0,2,"A new developer needs an account with a home directory and bash as the login shell. Which command creates it?",["usermod -m -s /bin/bash dev1","useradd -M -s /bin/bash dev1","useradd -m -s /bin/bash dev1","groupadd -m -s /bin/bash dev1"],2,"useradd -m creates the home directory and -s sets the shell. -M explicitly skips the home directory, and usermod changes an account that already exists.","2.0 Services and user management: local accounts",["usermod modifies an account that already exists; it will not create the new dev1 user.","useradd -M explicitly skips creating the home directory, which the developer needs.",null,"groupadd creates a group, not a user, and -m/-s are not valid groupadd options."]],["lx23",0,2,"User alice is in several supplementary groups and must also be added to docker without losing the others. Which command is correct?",["usermod -G docker alice","usermod -aG docker alice","groupadd docker alice","chgrp docker alice"],1,"-a (append) with -G adds the group and keeps existing ones. -G alone replaces her whole supplementary group list with just docker.","2.0 Services and user management: local accounts",["usermod -G without -a replaces alice's entire supplementary group list with just docker, dropping her other groups.",null,"groupadd creates a group; it does not add an existing user to one.","chgrp changes the group ownership of a file, not a user's group membership."]],["lx24",0,2,"Policy requires that alice change her password at least every 90 days. Which command enforces this?",["chage -E 90 alice","chage -M 90 alice","passwd -l alice","usermod -e 90 alice"],1,"chage -M sets the maximum password age in days. -E (and usermod -e) set an account expiry date, which disables the account rather than forcing a password change.","2.0 Services and user management: local accounts",["chage -E sets an account expiry date (here day 90 after the epoch), which disables the account rather than forcing a password change.",null,"passwd -l locks the account's password; it does not set a maximum password age.","usermod -e sets the account expiration date, not the password's maximum age."]],["lx25",0,2,"The /etc/shadow entry for alice begins \"alice:!$6$Qw...\". What does the ! before the hash mean?",["The password has already expired","The account password is locked","The account has no password set","The user must change it at next login"],1,"passwd -l or usermod -L adds ! in front of the hash so no password can match. An expired password is shown by the aging fields, not by a ! prefix.","2.0 Services and user management: local accounts",["An expired password is indicated by the aging fields in /etc/shadow, not by a ! placed before the hash.",null,"A password that is not set shows as an empty field (::), not a ! in front of an existing hash.","Forcing a change at next login is done with a 0 in the last-change field (chage -d 0), not with a ! prefix."]],["lx26",0,2,"Nginx has just been installed. It must start now and also start at every boot. Which single command does both?",["systemctl start nginx","systemctl unmask nginx","systemctl enable --now nginx","systemctl reload-or-restart nginx"],2,"enable --now creates the boot-time links and starts the unit immediately. start alone runs it now but it will not come back after a reboot.","2.0 Services and user management: systemd",["systemctl start runs the unit now but does not create the boot-time links, so it will not return after a reboot.","systemctl unmask only removes a mask; it neither starts nor enables the unit.",null,"reload-or-restart reloads or restarts a running unit and does not set it to start at boot."]],["lx27",0,2,"systemctl status shows: \"Loaded: loaded (/usr/lib/systemd/system/cups.service; disabled; preset: enabled)\" and \"Active: active (running)\". What is true?",["It starts at boot but is stopped right now","It is running now but will not start at boot","It is masked and cannot be started at all","It failed and systemd is restarting it"],1,"disabled describes boot-time behavior and active (running) the current state. The preset only says what the distribution's default would be.","2.0 Services and user management: systemd",["This reverses the fields: disabled describes boot behavior (won't start at boot) and active (running) describes the current state (running now).",null,"The unit is disabled, not masked; a masked unit would show 'masked' and could not be active.","The Active line shows active (running), not a failed or auto-restarting state."]],["lx28",0,2,"An admin must change a setting in a vendor-supplied systemd unit so the change survives package updates. What is the best method?",["Run systemctl edit to create a drop-in override","Edit the unit file in /usr/lib/systemd/system","Add the setting to the /etc/rc.local script","Copy the unit into /run/systemd and edit it"],0,"systemctl edit writes a drop-in under /etc/systemd/system/<unit>.d/ and reloads systemd. Files in /usr/lib/systemd/system are replaced by package updates, and /run is cleared at reboot.","2.0 Services and user management: systemd",[null,"Editing the file under /usr/lib/systemd/system works until the next package update replaces it, losing the change.","/etc/rc.local is a legacy boot script and is not the mechanism for overriding a systemd unit's settings.","Files in /run/systemd are cleared at every reboot, so the change would not persist."]],["lx29",0,2,"In a systemd .timer unit, which directive schedules the job to run once a day at midnight?",["OnBootSec=daily","OnCalendar=daily","ExecStart=daily","WantedBy=daily"],1,"OnCalendar sets wall-clock schedules, and daily means 00:00 every day. OnBootSec takes a time span after boot, not a calendar expression.","2.0 Services and user management: scheduling",["OnBootSec takes a time span measured after boot, not a calendar keyword like daily.",null,"ExecStart belongs in the service unit and specifies the command to run, not a schedule.","WantedBy sets install-time dependencies (which target pulls the unit in), not a run schedule."]],["lx30",0,2,"A crontab contains \"30 2 * * 1 /usr/local/bin/backup.sh\". When does the script run?",["At 01:30 every second day","At 02:30 on the 1st of every month","Every 30 minutes on Mondays after 2 a.m.","At 02:30 every Monday"],3,"The fields are minute, hour, day of month, month and day of week, so this is minute 30, hour 2, any date, any month, weekday 1 (Monday). The 1st of the month would go in the third field.","2.0 Services and user management: scheduling",["This misreads the fields: 30 2 is 02:30 and the trailing 1 is a weekday, not an every-other-day schedule.","The day-of-month field is *, and the trailing 1 is day-of-week (Monday), not the 1st of the month.","30 is minute 30 only, not an interval that runs every 30 minutes.",null]],["lx31",0,2,"A runaway process must be asked to shut down cleanly so it can flush its buffers. Which signal should be sent first?",["SIGKILL (9)","SIGSTOP (19)","SIGCONT (18)","SIGTERM (15)"],3,"SIGTERM asks the process to exit and can be handled for cleanup; it is the default for kill. SIGKILL cannot be caught, so the process gets no chance to clean up. Use it only if SIGTERM fails.","2.0 Services and user management: processes",["SIGKILL cannot be caught, so the process gets no chance to flush its buffers; it should be used only if SIGTERM fails.","SIGSTOP only suspends the process; it does not tell it to shut down and clean up.","SIGCONT resumes a stopped process; it does not stop or terminate one.",null]],["lx32",0,2,"A batch job with PID 4321 is slowing interactive users. Which command lowers its CPU priority without stopping it?",["renice -n -10 -p 4321","nice -n 10 4321","kill -STOP 4321","renice -n 10 -p 4321"],3,"A higher nice value means lower priority, so renice to 10 makes the job yield CPU. A negative value raises priority, and nice only starts new commands.","2.0 Services and user management: processes",["renice to a negative value raises the process's priority, making it use more CPU rather than less.","nice launches a new command with a priority; it cannot renice an existing PID, and 4321 is not a command.","kill -STOP freezes the process entirely instead of just lowering its CPU priority.",null]],["lx33",0,2,"A long-running command was started in the foreground. How can it be moved to the background without restarting it?",["Press Ctrl+C, then run bg","Press Ctrl+Z, then run bg","Press Ctrl+D, then run fg","Run nohup %1 from the prompt"],1,"Ctrl+Z suspends the job and bg resumes it in the background. Ctrl+C sends SIGINT and usually ends the job, so there is nothing left to resume.","2.0 Services and user management: processes",["Ctrl+C sends SIGINT and usually terminates the foreground job, leaving nothing to move to the background.",null,"Ctrl+D signals end-of-input, not job suspension, so there is no stopped job to bring to the foreground.","nohup launches a new command immune to SIGHUP; it does not background an already-running job by job number."]],["lx34",0,2,"On a RHEL-family server, the dig command is missing. Which command shows which package provides it?",["rpm -qa dig","dnf info dig","rpm -ql dig","dnf provides '*/bin/dig'"],3,"dnf provides searches repository metadata for the package that ships a file (bind-utils here). rpm -qa and rpm -ql query only packages that are already installed.","2.0 Services and user management: software management",["rpm -qa lists installed packages matching a name, but there is no installed package literally named dig.","dnf info dig looks up a package named dig, which does not exist (the file ships in bind-utils).","rpm -ql lists the files of an installed package named dig, which is not installed.",null]],["lx35",0,2,"After adding a new APT repository on Ubuntu, which command should run before installing a package from it?",["apt upgrade","apt autoremove","apt update","dpkg --configure -a"],2,"apt update downloads the package lists, including the new repository's. Without it, APT does not know the new packages exist. apt upgrade installs newer versions of packages already on the system.","2.0 Services and user management: software management",["apt upgrade installs newer versions of already-known packages; it does not fetch the new repository's package list.","apt autoremove removes unused dependencies and has nothing to do with refreshing package lists.",null,"dpkg --configure -a finishes configuring half-installed packages; it does not download package lists."]],["lx36",0,2,"An admin finds an unfamiliar /etc/ssh/sshd_config change and wants to know which installed RPM owns that file. Which command answers this?",["rpm -ql /etc/ssh/sshd_config","rpm -qi /etc/ssh/sshd_config","dnf search sshd_config","rpm -qf /etc/ssh/sshd_config"],3,"rpm -qf (query file) returns the owning package. rpm -ql lists the files in a package, so it expects a package name, not a file path.","2.0 Services and user management: software management",["rpm -ql lists the files contained in a package and expects a package name, not a file path.","rpm -qi shows package information and expects a package name, not a file path.","dnf search matches keywords in package names and summaries; it does not report which package owns a file.",null]],["lx37",0,2,"A container runs nginx on port 80 inside the container. Users must reach it on host port 8080. Which podman run option does this?",["-p 80:8080","--expose 8080","--network host:8080","-p 8080:80"],3,"The format is -p hostPort:containerPort. -p 80:8080 would publish host port 80 to a container port that nothing listens on.","2.0 Services and user management: containers",["-p 80:8080 maps host port 80 to container port 8080, where nothing is listening.","--expose only documents that a port is used; it does not publish or map it to the host.","--network host:8080 is invalid syntax; host networking shares the host stack and takes no port mapping.",null]],["lx38",0,2,"A database container loses its data whenever it is recreated from a new image version. What is the correct fix?",["Commit the running container to a new image every night","Keep the data in the container's writable layer","Raise the container's memory and CPU limits","Mount a volume for the data directory"],3,"Named volumes or bind mounts live outside the container's lifecycle, so the data survives when the container is replaced. The writable layer is deleted with the container.","2.0 Services and user management: containers",["Committing the container to an image nightly captures the image layers but does not reliably persist live data changes.","The container's writable layer is deleted whenever the container is removed, so the data is lost.","Raising CPU and memory limits does nothing to make the data survive a container replacement.",null]],["lx39",0,2,"Which command shows log messages for the sshd unit from the current boot only?",["journalctl -u sshd -b","journalctl -k -u sshd","dmesg --unit sshd","systemctl list-units sshd"],0,"-u filters by unit and -b limits output to the current boot. -k shows kernel messages only, which would hide sshd's own entries.","2.0 Services and user management: logging",[null,"-k restricts output to kernel messages, which would hide sshd's own service log entries.","dmesg shows the kernel ring buffer and has no --unit filter for systemd units.","systemctl list-units shows unit states, not the log messages a unit has produced."]],["lx40",0,3,"After running chmod 750 deploy.sh, what permission string does ls -l show for the file?",["-rwxr-xr-x","-rwxr-x---","-rw-r-x---","-rwx-w----"],1,"7 = rwx for the owner, 5 = r-x for the group, 0 = --- for others. -rwxr-xr-x would be 755.","3.0 Security: file permissions",["-rwxr-xr-x is 755, giving others r-x, but the final 0 means others get no permissions.",null,"-rw-r-x--- would make the owner 6 (rw-), but 7 means the owner has rwx.","-rwx-w---- would make the group 2 (-w-), but 5 means the group has r-x."]],["lx41",0,3,"A user's umask is 027. What permissions does a new regular file created with touch receive?",["-rw-r--r--","-rwxr-x---","-rw-rw----","-rw-r-----"],3,"Regular files start at 666 and the umask removes 027, leaving 640. Directories start at 777 and would get 750; that is where the x bits come from.","3.0 Security: file permissions",["-rw-r--r-- is 644, the result of umask 022; a umask of 027 also removes read from others.","Regular files do not get execute bits by default, since the 666 base has no execute bits for the umask to remove.","-rw-rw---- is 660 (umask 007), but 027 also removes the write bit from the group.",null]],["lx42",0,3,"ls -l /usr/bin/passwd shows -rwsr-xr-x root root. What does the s in the owner execute position mean?",["Only the owner can delete or rename it","It runs with the file owner's privileges","It is shared by every group on the system","It stays cached in memory after it exits"],1,"That is the SUID bit: the program runs with the owner's UID (root here), which lets passwd update /etc/shadow. Protection from deletion by other users is what the sticky bit does on directories.","3.0 Security: special permissions",["Restricting deletion or rename to the owner is the sticky bit on a directory, not the SUID bit.",null,"SUID has nothing to do with group sharing; group inheritance on directories is the SGID bit.","On modern Linux the sticky bit no longer keeps executables cached in memory."]],["lx43",0,3,"Users share /srv/shared, and everyone must be able to create files but must not delete other users' files. Which command sets this up?",["chmod 1777 /srv/shared","chmod 2777 /srv/shared","chmod 4777 /srv/shared","chmod 0777 /srv/shared"],0,"The leading 1 sets the sticky bit, so only a file's owner (or root) can delete or rename it, as on /tmp. 2 is setgid, which controls group inheritance, not deletion.","3.0 Security: special permissions",[null,"A leading 2 is setgid, which controls group inheritance on new files, not who may delete them.","A leading 4 is setuid, which is meaningless for this deletion-protection purpose on a directory.","0777 sets no special bit, so any user could delete any other user's files."]],["lx44",0,3,"Bob needs read access to report.pdf without changing its owner, group or other permissions. Which command grants it?",["setfacl -m u:bob:r report.pdf","getfacl -m u:bob:r report.pdf","chmod o+r report.pdf","chown bob report.pdf"],0,"setfacl -m adds an ACL entry for one user only. chmod o+r would let every user read the file, and getfacl only displays ACLs.","3.0 Security: access control lists",[null,"getfacl only displays existing ACLs, and -m is not one of its options.","chmod o+r grants read to every other user, not just to bob.","chown bob changes the file's owner, which alters more than just granting bob read access."]],["lx45",0,3,"On an SELinux-enforcing server, files moved from a home directory into /var/www/html return 403 errors, although the permissions look fine. What is the correct fix?",["setenforce 0","restorecon -Rv /var/www/html","chmod -R 777 /var/www/html","semanage port -a -t http_port_t -p tcp 80"],1,"mv keeps the original user_home_t label; restorecon resets the files to the default httpd_sys_content_t context. setenforce 0 only hides the problem by turning off enforcement.","3.0 Security: SELinux",["setenforce 0 disables SELinux enforcement, which hides the problem rather than fixing the incorrect file context.",null,"chmod -R 777 changes standard permissions, but the denial comes from the SELinux context, not permissions.","semanage port adds a port type label; it has nothing to do with the files' context under /var/www/html."]],["lx46",0,3,"Which command shows whether SELinux is currently Enforcing, Permissive or Disabled?",["aa-status","setsebool","ausearch","getenforce"],3,"getenforce prints the current mode (sestatus gives more detail). aa-status is the AppArmor equivalent and does not report SELinux.","3.0 Security: SELinux",["aa-status reports AppArmor's status, not the SELinux mode.","setsebool toggles SELinux booleans; it does not report the current mode.","ausearch searches the audit log for events; it does not print the current SELinux mode.",null]],["lx47",0,3,"On Ubuntu, an admin wants an AppArmor profile to log violations without blocking them while it is being tuned. Which command does this?",["aa-enforce","aa-disable","setenforce 0","aa-complain"],3,"aa-complain puts the profile in complain mode: violations are logged but allowed. aa-disable unloads the profile completely, so nothing is logged.","3.0 Security: AppArmor",["aa-enforce puts the profile in enforce mode, which blocks violations instead of only logging them.","aa-disable unloads the profile entirely, so violations are neither logged nor enforced.","setenforce 0 is an SELinux command, not AppArmor, and it disables enforcement system-wide.",null]],["lx48",0,3,"An admin ran firewall-cmd --add-service=https and HTTPS worked, but the rule disappeared after a reboot. What went wrong?",["firewalld does not support the https service","The rule was added without --permanent","The zone was set to trusted during boot","SELinux removed the rule at startup"],1,"Without --permanent, firewall-cmd changes only the runtime configuration. Add it with --permanent and then --reload (or use --runtime-to-permanent).","3.0 Security: firewalls",["firewalld does support the https service; it is a predefined service definition.",null,"A trusted zone would allow the traffic; it would not cause the rule to vanish after a reboot.","SELinux does not manage firewalld rules, so it did not remove the rule."]],["lx49",0,3,"An admin connected over SSH is about to turn on ufw for the first time. What must be done first to avoid being locked out?",["Run ufw allow OpenSSH","Run ufw default allow incoming","Run ufw reset, then ufw disable","Run ufw deny 22/tcp for brute force"],0,"ufw's default policy denies incoming traffic, so SSH must be allowed before ufw enable. Allowing all incoming traffic by default defeats the purpose of the firewall.","3.0 Security: firewalls",[null,"Setting the default incoming policy to allow opens the host to all inbound traffic, defeating the firewall.","Resetting and disabling ufw turns the firewall off, leaving the host unprotected.","Denying 22/tcp blocks SSH, which would immediately lock out the remote admin."]],["lx50",0,3,"Which sshd_config setting stops the root account from logging in directly over SSH?",["PasswordAuthentication yes","PermitRootLogin no","PermitEmptyPasswords no","UsePAM no"],1,"PermitRootLogin no blocks root logins, so admins sign in as themselves and use sudo. PermitEmptyPasswords no is good practice but does not stop root logging in with a password or key.","3.0 Security: SSH hardening",["PasswordAuthentication yes allows password logins in general but does not specifically stop the root account from logging in.",null,"PermitEmptyPasswords no blocks blank-password logins but still permits root to log in with a password or key.","UsePAM no disables PAM integration, which can break authentication features and does not block root logins."]],["lx51",0,3,"An admin generated an Ed25519 key pair and wants to use key-based login to server01. Which command installs the public key on the server?",["ssh-keygen -R server01","ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519","scp ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 admin@server01:","ssh-copy-id admin@server01"],3,"ssh-copy-id appends the public key to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys with the right permissions. Copying id_ed25519 would expose the private key, which should never leave the client.","3.0 Security: SSH hardening",["ssh-keygen -R removes a host's entry from known_hosts; it has nothing to do with installing your key on the server.","ssh-add loads a private key into the local agent; it does not place anything on the remote server.","Copying id_ed25519 sends the private key, which must never leave the client; only the .pub file belongs on the server.",null]],["lx52",0,3,"The ops group must be allowed to restart nginx with sudo, and nothing else. What is the safest way to add the rule?",["Append the rule to /etc/sudoers with echo","Add every member of ops to the wheel group","Create /etc/sudoers.d/ops with visudo -f","Edit /etc/sudoers directly in nano as root"],2,"visudo checks syntax before saving, so an error cannot break sudo, and a drop-in keeps the rule separate. Adding users to wheel grants full root, far more than needed.","3.0 Security: privilege escalation",["Appending with echo bypasses syntax checking, so a typo can break sudo for everyone.","Adding users to wheel grants full root privileges, far more than restarting nginx.",null,"Editing /etc/sudoers directly in nano skips validation, risking a syntax error that locks out sudo."]],["lx53",0,3,"On RHEL 9, security policy requires locking an account after five failed login attempts. Which PAM module provides this?",["pam_faillock","pam_limits","pam_pwquality","pam_env"],0,"pam_faillock counts failed attempts and locks the account (settings in /etc/security/faillock.conf). pam_pwquality enforces password complexity, not lockout.","3.0 Security: authentication",[null,"pam_limits enforces resource limits (ulimits) such as open files and processes, not lockout after failed logins.","pam_pwquality enforces password complexity rules, not account lockout on failed attempts.","pam_env sets environment variables at login and has nothing to do with lockout."]],["lx54",0,3,"An admin downloaded an ISO and a SHA256SUMS file from the vendor. Which command confirms the ISO matches the published checksum?",["gpg --symmetric SHA256SUMS","openssl rand -hex 32","sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS","md5sum --encrypt SHA256SUMS"],2,"sha256sum -c recomputes each hash and reports OK or FAILED. Verifying the GPG signature on SHA256SUMS (gpg --verify) proves who published it; --symmetric encrypts a file instead.","3.0 Security: cryptography",["gpg --symmetric encrypts a file with a passphrase; it does not verify a checksum.","openssl rand generates random bytes and has nothing to do with verifying a download.",null,"md5sum does not encrypt (there is no --encrypt), and it uses MD5 rather than SHA-256."]],["lx55",0,3,"A laptop's data partition /dev/sdb1 must be encrypted at rest with a passphrase that is asked for at boot. Which command starts this setup?",["cryptsetup luksFormat /dev/sdb1","gpg --encrypt /dev/sdb1","chattr +i /dev/sdb1","mdadm --encrypt /dev/sdb1"],0,"cryptsetup luksFormat creates a LUKS header on the partition. Then luksOpen, mkfs and an /etc/crypttab entry finish the setup. chattr +i makes a file immutable but does not encrypt anything.","3.0 Security: cryptography",[null,"gpg --encrypt encrypts files, not a whole block device that must be unlocked at boot.","chattr +i makes a file immutable; it does not encrypt anything.","mdadm manages software RAID and has no --encrypt option."]],["lx56",0,4,"Which statement best describes idempotence in an Ansible playbook?",["Every task runs on every host in parallel","Tasks run once and are then removed from the playbook","Each run rebuilds the host from a clean image","A second run changes nothing if the host already matches"],3,"Idempotent modules check the current state and act only when it differs, so reruns report ok instead of changed. Parallel execution across hosts is a separate feature (forks).","4.0 Automation: configuration management",["Running tasks on every host in parallel describes execution strategy (forks), not idempotence.","Playbooks are not consumed after running; they can be rerun at any time, so this is not idempotence.","Rebuilding a host from a clean image is reprovisioning, not the idempotent convergence of desired state.",null]],["lx57",0,4,"Which command checks that Ansible can reach and log in to every host in the web inventory group?",["ansible web -m ping","ansible-playbook web -m ping","ansible-galaxy ping web","ansible-vault ping web"],0,"The ansible command runs an ad hoc module against a pattern; the ping module confirms SSH access and a usable Python. ansible-playbook expects a playbook file, not -m.","4.0 Automation: configuration management",[null,"ansible-playbook requires a playbook file to run and does not accept an ad hoc -m module argument.","ansible-galaxy manages roles and collections; it has no ping capability.","ansible-vault encrypts and decrypts secrets; it does not test host connectivity."]],["lx58",0,4,"A playbook needs a database password, and the file will be committed to Git. Which tool should protect the secret?",["ansible-vault","ansible-doc","ansible-inventory","ansible-config"],0,"ansible-vault encrypts variable files or single strings with AES-256 and decrypts them at run time. The other tools display documentation, inventory or settings.","4.0 Automation: configuration management",[null,"ansible-doc displays module documentation; it does not encrypt secrets.","ansible-inventory displays and manages inventory; it does not protect secret values.","ansible-config views and manages Ansible settings; it does not encrypt secrets."]],["lx59",0,4,"Before changing cloud infrastructure defined in OpenTofu, the team wants to see what would be created, changed or destroyed. Which command shows this without applying anything?",["tofu apply -auto-approve","tofu destroy","tofu plan","tofu fmt"],2,"plan compares the configuration with the state and prints the proposed changes. apply -auto-approve makes the changes without asking for confirmation.","4.0 Automation: infrastructure as code",["apply -auto-approve makes the changes immediately without pausing for review.","destroy tears down the managed resources rather than previewing proposed changes.",null,"fmt only reformats configuration files; it does not show what would change."]],["lx60",0,4,"Which configuration management tool uses, by default, an agent on each node that pulls a compiled catalog from a central server?",["Puppet","Ansible","OpenTofu","Git"],0,"Puppet agents check in with the Puppet server and apply the catalog. Ansible is agentless and pushes changes over SSH.","4.0 Automation: configuration management",[null,"Ansible is agentless and pushes changes over SSH; it does not use a per-node agent pulling a catalog.","OpenTofu provisions infrastructure through state and providers; it is not a node agent applying a catalog.","Git is a version control system, not a configuration management agent."]],["lx61",0,4,"Running ./backup.sh returns \"Permission denied\" even though the owner can read the file. What fixes this?",["chmod u+x backup.sh","chown root backup.sh","export PATH=backup.sh","source ~/.bashrc"],0,"The script needs execute permission to run as ./backup.sh. Running bash backup.sh would also work because bash only needs to read the file.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting",[null,"chown root changes the file's owner; it does not add the missing execute permission.","export PATH=backup.sh corrupts the PATH variable and does nothing to make the script executable.","source ~/.bashrc reloads shell configuration and is unrelated to the file's execute bit."]],["lx62",0,4,"What does this print: grep -q root /etc/passwd; echo $?",["0","1","2","root"],0,"grep -q prints nothing and exits 0 when a match is found. It would be 1 for no match and 2 for an error such as a missing file.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting",[null,"An exit status of 1 is returned only when grep finds no match, but root does appear in /etc/passwd.","An exit status of 2 indicates an error such as a missing file, which is not the case here.","$? prints the numeric exit status, not the matched text, and -q suppresses grep's output entirely."]],["lx63",0,4,"APP_PORT is not set. What does echo \"${APP_PORT:-8080}\" print?",["APP_PORT","-8080","An empty line","8080"],3,"${var:-default} uses the default when the variable is unset or empty and does not assign it. ${var:=default} would also assign it.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting",["The variable name itself is never printed; parameter expansion substitutes a value.","The :- operator is not part of the output; only the default value is printed, without a leading dash.","With :- and an unset variable, the default is substituted, so the line is not empty.",null]],["lx64",0,4,"In a bash script, which test is true only when /opt/app exists and is a directory?",["[ -f /opt/app ]","[ -x /opt/app ]","[ -z /opt/app ]","[ -d /opt/app ]"],3,"-d tests for a directory. -f is true only for regular files, and -z tests whether a string is empty.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting",["-f is true only for regular files, so it would not confirm that /opt/app is a directory.","-x tests the executable bit, which does not confirm the path is a directory.","-z tests whether a string is empty and has nothing to do with file type.",null]],["lx65",0,4,"What does this print: for i in {1..3}; do printf \"%s\" $i; done; echo",["1 2 3","{1..3}","123","3"],2,"Brace expansion produces 1 2 3, and printf \"%s\" adds no spaces or newlines, so the output is 123 followed by the newline from echo.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting",["printf \"%s\" prints values with no separators, so no spaces appear between the numbers.","Brace expansion does occur, producing 1 2 3, so {1..3} is not printed literally.",null,"The loop iterates over every value and prints all of them, not just the last."]],["lx66",0,4,"A script runs curl ... | tee out.json and should stop if curl fails, but the pipeline always returns 0. Which option fixes this?",["set -x","set -u","set -o pipefail","set +e"],2,"By default a pipeline's exit status is the last command's (tee). pipefail returns the last non-zero status in the pipeline, so set -e can catch it. set -x only traces commands.","4.0 Automation: shell scripting",["set -x only traces commands as they run; it does not change a pipeline's exit status.","set -u makes unset variables an error and has no effect on the pipeline's exit status.",null,"set +e disables exit-on-error, which would ignore the failure rather than let it be caught."]],["lx67",0,4,"What does this Python print: print(len({1, 2, 2, 3}))",["4","2","3","A TypeError"],2,"Curly braces with values create a set, which drops the duplicate 2, leaving three elements. A list [1, 2, 2, 3] would have length 4.","4.0 Automation: Python",["A length of 4 would be correct for a list [1, 2, 2, 3], but braces create a set that drops the duplicate.","The set retains 1, 2 and 3, which is three elements, not two.",null,"This is valid syntax and len() works on a set, so no TypeError is raised."]],["lx68",0,4,"An AI assistant generated a shell script to delete old log files. What should the admin do before running it in production?",["Run it as root because AI output is pre-tested","Paste real credentials in to make it complete","Review it and test it on a non-production VM","Schedule it in cron before reading it through"],2,"Treat AI output like code from an unknown contributor: read it, test it somewhere safe and keep secrets out of prompts. A wrong path in a delete command can wipe data.","4.0 Automation: AI best practices",["AI output is not pre-tested, and running unreviewed code as root can cause serious, irreversible damage.","Real credentials should never be pasted into prompts or into untested scripts.",null,"Scheduling unreviewed deletion code in cron automates a potentially destructive mistake."]],["lx69",0,4,"A bad commit was already pushed to a shared main branch. Which command undoes it without rewriting history for other developers?",["git reset --hard HEAD~1, then force-push","git checkout -- .","git stash","git revert <commit>"],3,"git revert adds a new commit that reverses the bad one, so everyone else's history stays valid. reset plus force-push rewrites shared history and breaks other people's clones.","4.0 Automation: Git",["reset --hard followed by a force-push rewrites shared history and breaks other developers' clones.","git checkout -- . discards uncommitted local changes; it does not undo a commit that was already pushed.","git stash temporarily shelves local changes; it does not revert a committed change.",null]],["lx70",0,4,"Which command creates a new branch named feature/login and switches to it?",["git branch -d feature/login","git merge feature/login","git clone feature/login","git switch -c feature/login"],3,"git switch -c (or git checkout -b) creates the branch and moves HEAD to it. git branch -d deletes a branch.","4.0 Automation: Git",["git branch -d deletes an existing branch rather than creating and switching to a new one.","git merge integrates another branch into the current one; it does not create or switch branches.","git clone copies an entire remote repository; it is not used to create a local branch.",null]],["lx71",0,5,"Writes to /srv fail with \"No space left on device\", but df -h shows /srv only 40% used. df -i shows IUse% at 100%. What is the cause?",["The disk was remounted read-only","The user's memory limit was reached","The filesystem has run out of inodes","The partition table is corrupted"],2,"Every file uses an inode, and millions of tiny files can use them all while blocks are still free. A read-only mount gives \"Read-only file system\", not \"No space left\".","5.0 Troubleshooting: storage",["A read-only remount produces 'Read-only file system' errors, not 'No space left on device'.","A user memory limit does not cause disk write failures like this.",null,"A corrupted partition table would cause mount or read failures, not IUse% reaching 100%."]],["lx72",0,5,"df shows /var at 100%, but du -sh /var adds up to only 30% of the size. An old 20 GB log was deleted earlier. What is the most likely explanation?",["A running process still holds the deleted file open","du does not count files in hidden directories","The filesystem journal is using the space","The log was moved to swap by the kernel"],0,"Space is freed only when the last open handle closes. lsof +L1 shows deleted-but-open files; restart or signal the process to release the space. du does include hidden files.","5.0 Troubleshooting: storage",[null,"du does count files in hidden directories, so hidden files do not explain the gap.","The filesystem journal is small and would not account for a missing 20 GB.","Swap holds memory pages, not deleted disk files, so the log was not moved there."]],["lx73",0,5,"uptime on a 2-CPU server shows load average: 8.10, 7.95, 7.80. What does this indicate?",["Far more tasks are runnable or waiting than CPUs","The CPUs are each about 8% busy on average","Eight users are logged in at the moment","The server has been up for about eight days"],0,"Load average counts tasks that are running, runnable or in uninterruptible (often I/O) wait. A sustained load of about 8 on 2 CPUs means heavy queuing. It is not a CPU percentage.","5.0 Troubleshooting: performance",[null,"Load average is a count of tasks in the run queue, not a CPU-busy percentage.","Load average does not count logged-in users.","The length of time the server has been up is uptime, which is separate from the load numbers."]],["lx74",0,5,"A Java service keeps disappearing without writing an error to its own log. How can the admin confirm whether the kernel killed it for lack of memory?",["Check /etc/security/limits.conf for the service","Search the kernel log for OOM killer messages","Run free -h and look at the buff/cache column","Look for a core dump in the service's home"],1,"The OOM killer logs \"Out of memory: Killed process\" to the kernel ring buffer (journalctl -k or dmesg). free shows current memory use but not past kills.","5.0 Troubleshooting: performance",["/etc/security/limits.conf sets resource limits (ulimits); it does not record that a process was killed.",null,"free -h shows current memory use, not a past OOM-kill event.","The OOM killer does not leave a core dump by default, so its absence proves nothing."]],["lx75",0,5,"top shows low user CPU but a high wa (I/O wait) value. Which tool best shows which disk is saturated?",["iostat -x","lscpu","nproc","uname -r"],0,"iostat -x reports per-device utilization, queue size and await times (from the sysstat package). lscpu and nproc only describe CPU hardware.","5.0 Troubleshooting: performance",[null,"lscpu describes CPU hardware and topology, not disk I/O activity.","nproc only prints the number of processing units available.","uname -r shows the running kernel version and is unrelated to I/O saturation."]],["lx76",0,5,"A server can ping 8.8.8.8 but ping example.com fails with \"Temporary failure in name resolution\". What should be checked first?",["DNS settings in /etc/resolv.conf, tested with dig","The default gateway in the routing table","Whether the NIC driver module is loaded","Whether the firewall blocks outbound ICMP"],0,"Reaching an internet IP proves the NIC, gateway and ICMP all work. The failure is name resolution, so check the configured resolvers and test them with dig or resolvectl.","5.0 Troubleshooting: networking",[null,"Reaching 8.8.8.8 already proves the default gateway is working, so it is not the first thing to check.","The interface clearly works because IP traffic to 8.8.8.8 succeeds, so the NIC driver is not the issue.","ICMP to 8.8.8.8 succeeds, so the firewall is not blocking outbound ICMP."]],["lx77",0,5,"A new service fails to start with \"address already in use\" on port 8080. Which command shows which process already listens on that port?",["ip route show","ss -tulpn","ping -c 4 localhost","traceroute localhost"],1,"ss -tulpn lists TCP/UDP listening sockets with numeric ports and the owning process. ip route shows routing, not sockets.","5.0 Troubleshooting: networking",["ip route show displays the routing table, not which process is listening on a socket.",null,"ping localhost tests reachability of the loopback, not which process owns a port.","traceroute localhost traces a network path; it does not list listening sockets."]],["lx78",0,5,"A host can reach other machines on 192.168.10.0/24 but nothing beyond. ip route shows only \"192.168.10.0/24 dev eth0 proto kernel scope link src 192.168.10.20\". What is missing?",["A default route through the gateway","An IPv6 link-local address","A DNS server in /etc/hosts","A second address on eth0"],0,"With only the connected route, the kernel has no path to other networks. Add default via 192.168.10.1 (persistently with nmcli or netplan). DNS would not stop traffic sent to raw IP addresses.","5.0 Troubleshooting: networking",[null,"An IPv6 link-local address does not provide the missing IPv4 route to other networks.","Traffic sent to raw IP addresses does not need DNS, so a missing /etc/hosts entry would not block it.","A second address on eth0 would not create a path off the local subnet."]],["lx79",0,5,"systemctl status app shows \"Main process exited, code=exited, status=203/EXEC\". What is the most likely cause?",["The unit is masked by the administrator","Another process already holds its port","The ExecStart program is missing or not executable","The service ran out of memory at startup"],2,"Status 203/EXEC means systemd could not execute the command in ExecStart (wrong path, no execute bit or a bad shebang). A masked unit refuses to start and never reaches exec.","5.0 Troubleshooting: services",["A masked unit refuses to start and never reaches the point of executing ExecStart, so it would not show 203/EXEC.","A port already in use produces a runtime bind error inside the program, not the 203/EXEC exec failure.",null,"Running out of memory at startup shows a different status or a kill signal, not 203/EXEC."]],["lx80",0,5,"After a typo in /etc/fstab, the server boots into emergency mode with the root filesystem read-only. What is the correct recovery?",["Reinstall GRUB2 to the boot disk first","Run mkfs on the root filesystem","Remount / read-write and fix /etc/fstab","Boot to graphical.target and retry"],2,"mount -o remount,rw / makes the root filesystem writable so fstab can be corrected; then run systemctl daemon-reload and reboot. The boot loader is fine because the kernel already started.","5.0 Troubleshooting: boot",["The kernel has already booted, so the GRUB2 boot loader is fine and does not need reinstalling.","Running mkfs on the root filesystem would erase the entire system.",null,"Booting to graphical.target does not fix the fstab typo, which will still fail the mount."]],["lx81",0,5,"A server boots to a graphical desktop, and the admin wants it to boot to a text console by default from now on. Which command does this?",["systemctl set-default multi-user.target","systemctl isolate multi-user.target","systemctl set-default rescue.target","systemctl enable graphical.target"],0,"set-default changes the default.target link used at boot. isolate switches targets now but does not change what happens at the next boot.","5.0 Troubleshooting: boot",[null,"systemctl isolate switches to the target now but does not change what happens at the next boot.","set-default rescue.target boots into single-user rescue mode, not a normal multi-user text console.","Enabling graphical.target keeps the system booting into the GUI, the opposite of the goal."]],["lx82",0,5,"The kernel logs ext4 errors for the data partition /dev/sdb1, mounted on /data. What is the safe way to check and repair it?",["Run fsck /dev/sdb1 while it stays mounted","Run xfs_repair /dev/sdb1 to fix it","Run mkfs.ext4 /dev/sdb1 to clear errors","Unmount it, then run fsck /dev/sdb1"],3,"fsck (e2fsck) must run on an unmounted filesystem, or it can cause more corruption. xfs_repair is for XFS, and mkfs would erase the data.","5.0 Troubleshooting: storage",["Running fsck on a mounted filesystem can cause further corruption; it must be unmounted first.","xfs_repair is the tool for XFS filesystems, but this partition is ext4.","mkfs.ext4 reformats the partition, erasing all of its data.",null]],["lx83",0,5,"A web app on an SELinux-enforcing host is denied access to a file even after chmod 777. Where should the admin look to confirm the cause?",["ausearch -m avc for AVC denials","The output of ls -l on the file","The web server's access_log file","The /etc/sudoers.d directory"],0,"SELinux denials are logged as AVC records in the audit log; ausearch -m avc -ts recent shows them. Standard permissions are already wide open, so ls -l will not explain the denial.","5.0 Troubleshooting: security",[null,"ls -l shows standard permissions, which are already wide open at 777, so it cannot explain the denial.","The web server's access_log records HTTP requests, not SELinux access denials.","/etc/sudoers.d concerns sudo privilege rules and is unrelated to SELinux file access."]],["lx84",0,5,"ssh -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519 fails with \"Permissions 0644 for '/home/ana/.ssh/id_ed25519' are too open\". What fixes it?",["chmod 644 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub","chmod 777 ~/.ssh","chown root ~/.ssh/id_ed25519","chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519"],3,"The SSH client refuses a private key other users can read. 600 limits it to the owner. The .pub file can stay readable because it is public.","5.0 Troubleshooting: security",["Changing permissions on the .pub file misses the point; the private key, not the public one, is too open.","chmod 777 ~/.ssh makes the directory world-writable, which weakens security and does not fix the key.","chown root on the key would make the user unable to read their own private key.",null]],["lx85",0,5,"Kerberos and TLS logins on a server fail intermittently with clock-skew errors. Which command shows whether the host is synchronizing time and its current offset?",["hwclock --systohc","timedatectl set-timezone UTC","chronyc tracking","date +%s"],2,"chronyc tracking shows the reference source, stratum and system time offset. Changing the timezone does not fix skew, because UTC time is what matters to these protocols.","5.0 Troubleshooting: services",["hwclock --systohc writes the system time to the hardware clock; it does not show sync status or offset.","Setting the timezone to UTC does not change the underlying clock skew that these protocols care about.",null,"date +%s prints the current epoch time but reveals nothing about synchronization or offset."]],["lx86",0,5,"Installing a .deb with dpkg -i fails because dependencies are missing, leaving the package half-configured. Which command resolves it?",["apt --fix-broken install","dpkg --purge --force-all","apt-mark hold","apt autoremove"],0,"apt --fix-broken install (apt -f install) downloads the missing dependencies and finishes configuring the package. Forced purges can leave the system inconsistent.","5.0 Troubleshooting: software",[null,"dpkg --purge --force-all forcibly removes packages and can leave the system in an inconsistent state.","apt-mark hold pins a package to its current version; it does not resolve missing dependencies.","apt autoremove removes unused packages; it does not install the missing dependencies."]],["lx87",0,5,"Users report a slow server. Which command lists the processes using the most CPU, highest first?",["ps aux --sort=-%cpu | head","ps aux --sort=-%mem | head","free -m | head","lsof | head"],0,"--sort=-%cpu sorts by CPU usage in descending order. Sorting by %mem finds memory hogs instead, which does not match the symptom.","5.0 Troubleshooting: performance",[null,"--sort=-%mem sorts by memory use rather than CPU, so it does not match the reported symptom.","free -m shows overall memory totals, not per-process CPU usage.","lsof lists open files, not CPU consumption by process."]],["lx88",0,5,"dmesg shows repeated I/O errors and read retries on /dev/sda. Which command reports the drive's own health counters, such as reallocated sectors?",["fdisk -l /dev/sda","smartctl -a /dev/sda","blkid /dev/sda","hdparm -z /dev/sda"],1,"smartctl (smartmontools) reads SMART attributes and self-test results from the drive. fdisk -l shows only the partition layout.","5.0 Troubleshooting: hardware",["fdisk -l shows only the partition layout, not the drive's SMART health counters.",null,"blkid reports the UUID and filesystem type, not SMART attributes.","hdparm -z re-reads the partition table; it does not report drive health."]],["lx89",0,5,"A branch office can reach its own gateway but not app01 at headquarters. Which tool shows the hop where packets stop?",["arp -a","hostname -I","traceroute app01","ethtool eth0"],2,"traceroute (or tracepath / mtr) lists each router hop until replies stop, which shows where the path breaks. arp -a only shows local Layer 2 neighbors.","5.0 Troubleshooting: networking",["arp -a shows local Layer 2 neighbors on the same segment, not the remote path to headquarters.","hostname -I prints the host's own IP addresses and reveals nothing about the path.",null,"ethtool eth0 shows the local NIC's link settings, not where along the path packets stop."]],["lx90",0,5,"podman run -p 8080:80 web fails with \"address already in use\". What is the cause?",["The image does not expose port 80 in its metadata","Rootless containers cannot publish any ports","Another process already listens on host port 8080","Port 80 inside the container is already taken"],2,"The host side of the mapping is what conflicts; each container has its own network namespace, so port 80 inside is free. Rootless podman can publish ports at 1024 and above.","5.0 Troubleshooting: containers",["Port publishing works regardless of whether the image's metadata exposes port 80.","Rootless podman can publish ports at 1024 and above, so this is not the cause.",null,"Each container has its own network namespace, so port 80 inside the container is free; the conflict is on the host side."]],["lx91",0,5,"After a kernel update, a server fails to boot, but the previous kernel still works. What is the quickest way to get it running while investigating?",["Reinstall the OS from the installation media","Select the previous kernel from the GRUB menu","Run dracut -f from the firmware setup screen","Delete /boot and reboot with the old disk"],1,"GRUB keeps older kernels as menu entries. Boot the known-good one, then fix or remove the new kernel (and set the default with grubby or grub-set-default). Reinstalling is unnecessary.","5.0 Troubleshooting: boot",["Reinstalling the OS is unnecessary because the previous kernel still boots the system fine.",null,"The firmware setup screen cannot run dracut; rebuilding the initramfs requires a booted system.","Deleting /boot removes the kernels and boot files, making the system harder to recover, not easier."]]]);
